==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|fr|publicité}}, From {{der|en|ML.|pūblicitātem}}, accusative singular of {{m|la|pūblicitās}}, from {{der|en|la|pūblicus||public, general}}.

Morphologically {{suffix|en|public|ity|nocat=1}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|en|ɪsɪti|s=4}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|+}}

# [[advertise|Advertising]] or other activity designed to [[rouse]] public interest in something.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1963|month=February|title=Nobody runs this railway, mate|journal=Modern Railways|page=73|text=Any '''publicity''', runs the axiom, is good '''publicity'''.}}
# Public interest attracted in this way.
# The condition of being the object of public attention.
# The quality of being public, not private.
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Vanity Fair|passage=Amelia's gentle eyes, too, had been fixed anxiously on the pair, whose conduct had so chafed the jealous General; but when Rebecca entered her box, she flew to her friend with an affectionate rapture which showed itself, in spite of the '''publicity''' of the place; for she embraced her dearest friend in the presence of the whole house, at least in full view of the General's glass, now brought to bear upon the Osborne party.}}

